s` the entry is expired: it is removed and the URL is fetched again.\nR3. A response with any other status is returned but never stored. If `fetchImpl` rejects, `get` rejects with the same error and nothing is stored.\nR4. While a fetch for a URL is in progress, other calls to `get` for the same URL (fragment ignored) wait for that fetch and get its result (or its rejection) instead of starting another one. This holds whatever `ttlMs` is. Once the fetch has finished, the next call starts afresh.\nR5. `get(url, { fresh: true })` never reads the cache and never waits for a fetch already in progress: it always calls `fetchImpl`. If the response is a 2xx one it replaces the stored entry (when `ttlMs > 0`); otherwise the stored entry is left alone.\nR6. `clear(url)` removes the stored entry for that URL (fragment ignored), and `clear()` with no argument removes every entry. Neither affects fetches in progress or the statistics. `stats()` returns a new object `{ hits, misses, fetches }`: `hits` counts the calls of `get` answered from the cache, `misses` counts every other call of `get` that got past the URL check (including `fresh` calls and calls that waited for a fetch in progress), and `fetches` counts the calls made to `fetchImpl`.\nR7. A response returned by `get` is a deep copy: changing anything inside it, at any depth (its `status`, its `body`, or anything within the `body`), must not change what is stored or what any other caller received, including callers that shared the same fetch. The URL check from before stays: a `url` that is not a non-empty string throws a `TypeError` (as a rejected promise, since `get` is async).\n\nWhen all the rules hold, reply with the single word: done"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"reasoning_content":"I'll start by looking at what's already in the project.\n","tool_calls":[{"id":"M3YrIHTbVgORsYBQehaXtMh8NCv02pzY","type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"find /tmp/endo-experiment-5c78d675047f/s